/**
 * # Integrated Modelling worldview
 *
 * The root introduces the shared vocabulary of observation, the configurations
 * through which systems are recognized, and the domains that organize the
 * worldview. Its declarations are universals. Models and digital twins supply
 * particular entities, observations and bindings between instances.
 *
 * Read the opening sections in order: system configurations establish a common
 * organizational vocabulary; physical anchors identify subject matter; named
 * Knowledge domains introduce descriptive perspectives on that subject matter.
 * The observation kinds and general predicates that follow are shared by the
 * disciplinary namespaces. The strawman supplies a coherent starting vocabulary,
 * while leaving detailed scientific articulation to subsequent community work.
 *
 * ## Three distinct semantic relations
 *
 * `about` identifies a Knowledge domain's physical subject matter. `describes`
 * relates a description to its subject, including a Domain describing a
 * Configuration. `of` specifies bearer inherency. Selecting a domain does not
 * make every owned quality inhere in that domain's subject matter, and it does
 * not turn subjects, events or qualities into Configurations.
 *
 * ## Observational scope
 *
 * The root worldview seeks broad adoption through minimal explicit commitments
 * that support shared observations and leave room for differing scientific
 * approaches. Models establish which components matter in an observational
 * context. In k.LAB, a system's relevant parts depend on the question, scale and
 * way it is observed. Specific observational relations can express their meanings
 * and intended inferences without descending from a generic "part-of" relation.
 *
 * The literature helps distinguish meanings that broad part-of language can
 * obscure. Components of objects, members of collections and portions of masses
 * differ from spatial inclusion; mixing these relations can produce apparently
 * invalid inference chains without showing that parthood is generally
 * nontransitive. [Winston, Chaffin and Herrmann (1987)](https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1207/s15516709cog1104_2)
 * Formal accounts also distinguish object parthood over time, temporal parts of
 * events, constitution and persistence. [Borgo et al. (2022)](https://www.loa.istc.cnr.it/wp-content/uploads/2021/11/DOLCE_FOUST_2022.pdf)
 * These distinctions inform our design; they do not dictate one architecture.
 *
 * The initial vocabulary also omits a formal disposition taxonomy. Dispositions
 * differ from their manifestations and may never be realized; formal
 * representation requires explicit commitments about bearers, realizations and
 * relevant conditions. [Röhl and Jansen (2011)](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/2041-1480-2-S4-S4)
 * Unobserved capacities need not be unreal. The scope choice serves a coherent
 * observational vocabulary, without rejecting dispositions as explanatory
 * concepts.
 *
 * Ordinary component, constituent and capability descriptions remain available,
 * with specific observational relationships expressed on their own terms.
 *
 * ## Reading and writing this worldview
 *
 * A quoted declaration docstring gives the concept's meaning. Literate Markdown
 * comments explain the scope, sequence and rationale of related declarations;
 * they should read as a connected account rather than repeat every docstring.
 * Use qualified names in backticks when referring to concepts, and ordinary
 * Markdown links for sources that support a specific claim or design decision.
 * Explain conceptual distinctions where they help the reader interpret a
 * declaration, and keep each namespace's account within its stated scope.
 *
 * The numbered ODO 0.1.1 foundation supplies the imported kinds. The root's
 * aliases expose those kinds in the worldview vocabulary; the named domains and
 * system configurations organize their use in the namespaces that follow.
 */
ontology imod
   "Shared observation concepts, system configurations and domain interfaces for the Integrated Modelling worldview."
   version 1.0
   in domain root 
      with core 
         "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/integratedmodelling/odo-im/master/releases/0.1.1/odo.owl" as odo
   metadata {
      dc.creator: (
         "Ferdinando Villa (ferdinando.villa@bc3research.org)"
         "Greta Adamo (greta.adamo@bc3research.org)"
      )
   };


/**
 * # Systems and Earth-system organization
 *
 * The core alias precedes the ordinary configurations that use it. System is
 * generic; EarthSystem supplies the Earth-specific intermediate. Sphere
 * configurations may overlap and are neither exhaustive nor disjoint.
 *
 * PhysicalSystem is retained as a universal specialization of System, without
 * an intrinsic Earth restriction. EarthSystem specializes PhysicalSystem; its six
 * sphere configurations retain their existing PhysicalSystem ancestry.
 * PhysicalSystem is a Configuration, distinct from a Physical domain. It need not
 * be the parent of every physical phenomenon.
 *
 * System qualification or inherency belongs on physical-domain expressions.
 * Knowledge domains identify physical subject matter with about, rather than
 * inhering in a system Configuration. Configuration description with describes
 * is a separate relation; it does not supply ABOUT grounding or bind instances.
 */
abstract configuration Configuration
    "Configurations represent emergent patterns (aggregates of other entities) that are generated by observations of 
     quality and relationship."
   is core odo:Configuration;

abstract configuration System
    "An observationally recognized configuration expressing the organization of
     interacting parts and their relationships through identifiable patterns."
    is imod:Configuration;

configuration PhysicalSystem
    "A system configuration expressing organization of physical phenomena at a
     stated observational scope, without an intrinsic restriction to Earth."
    is imod:System;

configuration EarthSystem
    "A system configuration expressing organization of Earth phenomena at a
     stated observational scope, including their interactions."
    is imod:PhysicalSystem
    has children
        Atmosphere
            "Earth-system organization of atmospheric gases, layers and exchanges.",
        Hydrosphere
            "Earth-system organization of water in its physical settings and states.",
        Geosphere
            "Earth-system organization of solid materials, structures and interactions.",
        Biosphere
            "Earth-system organization of living organisms and their environmental interactions.",
        Anthroposphere
            "Earth-system organization of human populations, activities and social relationships.",
        Technosphere
            "Earth-system organization of human-made artifacts and built networks.";

/**
 * # Domain kinds and physical subject matter
 *
 * The four core aliases expose imported kinds; they have no additional clauses.
 * Physical, Knowledge and Normative are pairwise disjoint and non-exhaustive.
 * Only ordinary physical-domain specializations carry system qualification here.
 * Knowledge domains use `about` to identify physical subject matter, independently
 * of configuration description and the bearers of their owned qualities.
 *
 * An ontology may select the exact generic Knowledge category when its vocabulary
 * spans physical subjects, as in data's observation-scaling predicates. A named
 * specialization must instead identify explicit or inherited physical subject
 * matter, even when abstract. Concrete Knowledge-owned qualities still need an
 * effective description; generic category ownership does not waive that contract.
 */
abstract domain Domain
    "The field or perspective within which owned concepts are articulated."
    is core odo:Domain;

abstract domain Physical
    "A domain articulating physical phenomena."
    is core odo:PhysicalDomain;

abstract domain Knowledge
    "A descriptive or explanatory domain whose specializations identify physical subject matter."
    is core odo:KnowledgeDomain;

abstract domain Normative
    "A domain articulating prescriptions, requirements or permissions within an identifiable framework."
    is core odo:NormativeDomain;

/**
 * # Shared physical anchors
 *
 * These anchors provide subject matter, not full disciplinary taxonomies.
 * PhysicalPhenomena, Life, Society and BuiltEnvironment are universal in scope;
 * Earth alone introduces Earth-system scope. Their system qualification does not
 * bind every owned quality to one bearer or assert a shared particular witness.
 * About and describes remain independent relations.
 */
domain PhysicalPhenomena
    "Physical phenomena considered through physical-system organization, without an intrinsic restriction to Earth."
    is imod:Physical of imod:PhysicalSystem
    describes imod:PhysicalSystem;

domain Earth
    "Physical Earth phenomena considered within Earth-system organization."
    is imod:PhysicalPhenomena of imod:EarthSystem
    describes imod:EarthSystem;

domain Life
    "Living phenomena and their physical settings, without an intrinsic restriction to Earth."
    is imod:PhysicalPhenomena;

domain Society
    "Observed social phenomena and collective organization, without an intrinsic restriction to Earth."
    is imod:PhysicalPhenomena;

domain BuiltEnvironment
    "Built artifacts and networks in their settings, uses and functioning, without an intrinsic restriction to Earth."
    is imod:PhysicalPhenomena;

/**
 * # Named descriptive domains
 *
 * Each ordinary specialization names a descriptive perspective and explicitly
 * identifies physical subject matter. Repeated `about` modifiers retain separate
 * links: Ecology concerns both Life and PhysicalPhenomena, while Land connects
 * Earth, Society and Life. `about (A and B)` would instead name one composite
 * target. These declarations add necessary constraints, not reverse definitions,
 * a shared instance witness, or a computation.
 *
 * Geography describes Earth locations, terrain and regional distinctions.
 * Valuation describes societal preferences across many contexts, including
 * economic, ethical, aesthetic and health concerns. Describing a preference is
 * different from prescribing what ought to be valued. A Normative domain
 * identifies the framework within which prescriptions are articulated.
 *
 * `AtmosphereKnowledge` distinguishes the descriptive domain from the existing
 * `Atmosphere` Configuration. Both names are deliberate: one identifies a
 * perspective on Earth phenomena, the other their recognized system organization.
 */
domain Agency
    "Observed agency, action and interaction in their social setting."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:Society;

domain AtmosphereKnowledge
    "Descriptive and explanatory knowledge of Earth atmospheric phenomena."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:Earth;

domain Biology
    "Descriptive and explanatory knowledge of living structure, function and occurrences."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:Life;

domain Chemistry
    "Descriptive and explanatory knowledge of composition and chemical transformations, without an intrinsic restriction to Earth."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:PhysicalPhenomena;

domain Ecology
    "Descriptive and explanatory knowledge of living interactions and their physical settings."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:Life about imod:PhysicalPhenomena;

domain Economics
    "Descriptive and explanatory knowledge of observed production, exchange and accounting."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:Society;

domain Engineering
    "Descriptive and explanatory knowledge of built artifacts and their observed operation."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:BuiltEnvironment;

domain Genetics
    "Descriptive and explanatory knowledge of genomic bearers, inheritance and molecular occurrences."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:Life;

domain Geography
    "Descriptive and explanatory knowledge of Earth locations, terrain and spatial organization, including geographical and regional identities."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:Earth;

domain Geology
    "Descriptive and explanatory knowledge of Earth geological bodies, materials and occurrences."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:Earth;

domain Hydrology
    "Descriptive and explanatory knowledge of Earth water bodies, flow and storage."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:Earth;

domain Land
    "Descriptive knowledge of Earth land cover, use and management; physical, social and living subject matter remain distinct."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:Earth about imod:Society about imod:Life;

domain Oceanography
    "Descriptive and explanatory knowledge of Earth marine bodies and their conditions."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:Earth;

domain Physics
    "Descriptive and explanatory knowledge of physical quantities and processes, without an intrinsic restriction to Earth."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:PhysicalPhenomena;

domain Sociology
    "Descriptive and explanatory knowledge of observed social relations and processes."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:Society;

domain Soil
    "Descriptive and explanatory knowledge of Earth soils, horizons and their physical settings."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:Earth;

domain Valuation
    "Descriptive knowledge of societal preferences and value attribution across economic, ethical, aesthetic, health and other contexts, including valued assets and their associated hazards and risks."
    is imod:Knowledge about imod:Society;


//Identity
abstract identity Identity
    "Identity specifies the assignment of a concept to a certain class of concepts based on 
     a series of characteristics, such as morphological, behavioral and contextual"
   is core odo:Identity;
   

//Identities capturing discrete space for the worldview

abstract identity Puntal; 
abstract identity Lineal; 
abstract identity Areal; 
abstract identity Volumetric;
abstract identity Outcome
     "Final output of a Process";


//Attribute

abstract attribute Attribute
    "Attribute specifies descriptive accidental predicates of a subject."
   is core odo:Attribute;

abstract ordering Evidence
     "Characterizes degrees of evidence for an hypothesis."   
     has children 
         Possible "Hypothesis cannot be dismissed based on reasoning although no documented supporting observations exist.",
         Certain "No doubt can remain about evidence - self-evident or blatant truth",
         Hypothetical "Hypothesis can be made in support of an interpretation although no documented supporting observations exist. Similarly valid counter-arguments may exist.",
         Observed "Degree of evidence is supported by documented observations, but still  evidence is indirect.";

abstract subjective ordering Coarseness 
   has children
      Fine,
      Coarse;

abstract subjective ordering Level 
      "Two-level hierarchy for generic discretizations." 
      has children  
      (Low 
            has children 
              Minimal, 
              ExtremelyLow, 
              VeryLow, 
              ModeratelyLow
             ),
        Moderate, 
       (High 
            has children
              ModeratelyHigh,
              VeryHigh,
              ExtremelyHigh,
              Maximal
            );
   
//Realm
   
abstract realm Realm
    "Realm specifies the physical (e.g. of the biosphere) location of a concept"
   is core odo:Realm;

//Role

abstract role Role
    "Role specifies predicates that can be played by a concept in a context"
   is core odo:Role;
   
/**
 * Observable
 */

//Process
abstract process Process
     "A dynamic occurrence unfolding over time, typically open-ended (no temporal boundaries) and homogeneous in nature."
   is core odo:Process;
    
//Event
     
abstract event Event
    "An event is an independent occurrent that happens in time, close-ended (with temporal boundaries) and with participants."
   is core odo:Event;
       
@todo("should have some articulation of the 'Outcome' part")
@todo("see note above for the 'requires identity' clause")
deniable attribute Positive
    "Describes the positive or negative outcome of an event or a subject."
    ;

@todo("Must articulate relationship vs. bond and functional vs. structural, as these are fundamental and disjoint.")   
abstract relationship Relationship
      "A relationship directionally connects two subjects or agents."
    is core odo:Relationship
    ;

abstract configuration Network 
   "A network is a configuration of subjects connected by relationships."
   is imod:Configuration of imod:Relationship
   has children 
      (abstract SpatialNetwork  
      ),
      (
      abstract IntentionalNetwork
      inherits imod:Outcome  
      );

/**
 * Countable
 */

abstract thing Subject
      	"Subject is a Substantial without agentive characteristics"
      	is core odo:Subject;

/**
 * Agent nature concerns capacity for action, rather than merely undergoing
 * change. An agent is recognizable as a unit for interaction; its specialization
 * establishes which actions and aspects of its own evolution it can determine.
 * Deliberative, interactive and reactive are optional characterizations, not
 * prerequisites of Agent nature.
 */
abstract agent Agent
      	"A substantial capable of acting on other entities or itself, including determining aspects of its own observable evolution."
      	is core odo:Agent;
     
/**
 * Qualities
 */
abstract quality Quality
     "Qualities represent observables that cannot be observed directly but only through other entities that bear them.
      Their observation produces 'values' in some reference domain. They inhere to other entities and depend on their bearers."
     is core odo:Quality;

abstract quantity Quantity
    "Quantity are qualities whose values are quantified numerically."     
   is core odo:QuantifiableQuality;
   
abstract duration Duration 
   is core odo:Duration;
   
//Space qualities
//Comment: re-think "observation" ontology and below concepts
@fixme("Decide what to do with the spatial geometry identities. They can be here w/o referring to ODO (probably best) although
        they should refer to klab: namespace as they're needed in the software.")
abstract area Area 
   is core odo:Area 
   ;

abstract length Length
   is core odo:Length 
   ;       




//Numerosity

abstract quantity Numerosity     
   is core odo:Numerosity;


/**
 * Intensive physical properties 
 */
 

abstract angle Angle 
   "Geometric property that measures the amount of rotation needed to bring one line or plane into coincidence with another. 
   Typically measured in degrees or radians."   
   is core odo:Angle;

abstract energy Energy
   "The capacity of a system to perform work or produce heat. Typically measured in joules (J)." 
   is core odo:Energy;

abstract temperature Temperature
   "Measure of the average kinetic energy of the particles in a substance or system. 
   Typically measured with scales such as Celsius, Fahrenheit, or Kelvin."
   is core odo:Temperature;

resistivity Resistivity 
   "A measure of a material's resistance to the flow of electric current. 
   It quantifies how strongly a material opposes the flow of electrical charges and is typically measured in ohm-meters."
   is core odo:Resistivity;

abstract velocity Velocity
   "Quantity that describes the rate of change of an object's position with respect to time. 
   Typically measured in units such as meters per second (m/s) or kilometers per hour (km/h)." 
   is core odo:Speed;

abstract mass Mass
   "Property of matter that quantifies the amount of substance in an object. 
    Typically measured in units such as kilograms (kg) or grams (g)."
   is core odo:Mass 
   ;

abstract amount Amount
   "Refers to a quantitative measure or quantity of something. 
    It represents the magnitude or volume of a substance, object, or phenomenon, and can be expressed numerically or qualitatively."
   is core odo:Amount
   ;

abstract volume Volume
   "Measure of the three-dimensional space occupied by an object or substance. It quantifies the amount of space enclosed by the boundaries 
   of the object or substance and is typically measured in cubic units such as cubic meters or cubic centimeters." 
   is core odo:Volume
   ;
   
abstract weight Weight
   "The force exerted on an object due to gravity. 
   Typically expressed in units of force such as newtons (N) or pounds-force (lbf)."  
   is core odo:Weight  
   ;
   
abstract quantity Proportion
   "The relative size, quantity, or magnitude of one element compared to another within a given context. 
   Can be expressed numerically, often as a fraction, percentage, or ratio."       
   is core odo:Proportion;

abstract quantity Ratio
   "The quantitative relationship between two quantities or amounts, typically expressed as the quotient of one quantity divided by another. It indicates the proportion or comparative magnitude of one quantity relative to another. 
    Ratios can be represented in various forms, such as fractions, decimals, or percentages."       
   is core odo:Ratio;


//Category
   
abstract class Type 
    "Type defines categories"        
   is core odo:EnumerableQuality; 

//Priority

abstract priority Priority
   "Relative importance or precedence assigned to entities or actions within a system or context, often based on predefined criteria or constraints." 
   is core odo:Priority;

abstract priority Concordance
"Conceptual distance from an idea or theory"
;

attribute Comparable
   "Describes the ability to rank an object over another one."
   deniable as Incomparable
   applies to imod:Concordance;

role Reference
    "Applied to an observation used as reference to compute the primary observation by comparison. 
     Can be linked to a rescaling filters to shift the context of observation made in different space and time."
    ;

role Indicator 
   applies to imod:Quantity
   has children   
     (AbsoluteIndicator
     "An absolute indicator has values that range between a known minimum and maximum. Its value corresponds 
      to a state that is fully understood in terms of the indicator."  
       ),
     (RelativeIndicator 
     "A relative indicator can only compare two states of the same entity within the same context of observation.
      It does not suggest any absolute value for the state it refers to."
     );

//Value

abstract money MonetaryValue
    "Quantitative representation of the economic worth or exchangeable value assigned to goods, services, or assets, typically denoted in a currency unit."
    is core odo:MonetaryValue;
    
abstract quantity PreferenceValue
   "Quantitative or qualitative measure representing the degree of desirability, favorability, or priority assigned to an option, choice, or outcome within a decision-making context."
   is core odo:PreferenceValue;
   
abstract role ValueObject
     "An entity that embodies, represents, or holds value, which may include but is not limited to monetary, conceptual, or symbolic value."
     applies to imod:Subject;
     
abstract relationship ValuationRelationship
     "Connection or association between entities or concepts that involves the assessment, determination, or assignment of value."
     links imod:Agent to imod:Subject;

attribute Regular
    "State or condition characterized by consistency, orderliness, or adherence to established patterns or rules."
    deniable as Irregular;

abstract quality Heterogeneity 
	;
         
//Extent


//Temporal extent
            
abstract extent Time 
     is core odo:Time 
     ;
    
//Spatial extent
 
 abstract extent Space 
    "Space in the IM worldview is 0, 1,2 or 3-dimensional, with implied traits that describe human perception and modalities of description proper of an Euclidean view of the world."
    is core odo:Space
            ;